<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lori</h1><p class="st-meta">979 CE – 1118 CE · capital Lori</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Armenian Bagratid kingdom in the gorges of northern Armenia, known for the monasteries of Haghpat and Sanahin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurgen-i-of-lori">Gurgen</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-iii-of-armenia">Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia</a>, was given the northern provinces of Tashir and Dzoraget and took the title king, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiurikian-dynasty">Kyurikian</a> line, named after his Georgian-style name Kyurike. The kingdom straddled the borderlands between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled from fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samshvilde">Samshvilde</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lori-berd">Lori</a>, controlling routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>. The royal family endowed the monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanahin-monastery">Sanahin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haghpat-monastery">Haghpat</a>, founded by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrovanuysh">Khosrovanuysh</a> in the 960s and 970s, which became major centres of learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copying. The kingdom survived the fall of Ani in 1045 by allying with neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Seljuk attacks reduced the kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lori-berd">Lori</a> and its gorges, and it paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. In 1118 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a> took Lori; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiurikian-dynasty">Kyurikians</a> later held Tavush and Matsnaberd in the north-east.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-lori.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Lori" width="320" height="265"><figcaption>Image: www.armenica.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jorgat_harasm.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>979 CE – 1118 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lori</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gurgen, David Anhoghin, Kyurike II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Tashir-Dzoraget, Kyurikian Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1048&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lori" aria-label="Kingdom of Lori on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5767 714 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lori lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6087 970L6069 986M6087 970L6481 1127M6087 970L6036 930"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6069 986h0M6481 1127h0M6036 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6087 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6087 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1048&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lori">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
